INT. FUNERAL PARLOR - VIEWING ROOM - NIGHT

Folding chairs. Carnations in a wire stand. A closed casket, brass handles dull.

JOSEPHINE "JO" ADEBAYO-KRUSE (40s, black coat, sunglasses she doesn't remove) sits in the front row. She does not turn when the door opens.

MALACHI TREVELYAN-SHAW (50s, black suit, damp at the shoulders) stands in the doorway. He holds a small paper bag.

He walks the aisle. Sits two chairs from her. Sets the bag on the empty seat between them.

She looks at it. Doesn't touch it.

A phone rings in her coat. She lets it go three rings. He watches her hand.

She takes it out. Answers. Holds it between them on speaker.

VOICE (V.O.)
(over the phone)
Is he there?

JO
He's here.

VOICE (V.O.)
Then you know.

She says nothing. Her thumb moves along the edge of the phone. He leans his elbows on his knees and looks at the casket.

VOICE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Open the bag.

She doesn't. He does. Takes out a single brass key. Holds it up to the lamp. The key turns slowly on its string.

VOICE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
That's the end of it.

Jo hangs up. Slips the phone back in her coat. She takes the key from his fingers, and for a moment their hands stay together.

She stands. Smooths her coat. He stays seated. She walks to the casket and lays the key on the lid, beside the carnations.

At the door she stops. He still hasn't looked at her. She goes out, and he sits on, staring at the key.
